    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6819",
      "problem": "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below. Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/1fcbb5677ad7c8f630ccd7d194ef92f59fe3424d6bdb85e20f1ae522567b94ec.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) <             0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ So, $f''(x) >  0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/1567dcdd3dac0eb6b76b71e20466a77457a3d956c85be96048e5d3a94568c03a.png)",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/observations/step-e6819-eb255a17814c7c8effb6.html",
